.page scp 1 copy files to or from another machine, securely

# SYNOPSIS
*scp* [*-Bpqrv*] [*-F* _FILE_] [*-i* _FILE_] [*-o* _OPTION_] [*-P* _PORT_] _SOURCE_ ... _TARGET_

# DESCRIPTION
`scp` copies files between this machine and one other over an encrypted SSH connection (the SFTP protocol, which every current SSH server has: the old way, in which a remote scp was started and fed through the shell, is not used). A remote name is `[user@]host:path`; a relative path starts in the home directory on the far side and an empty one is the home directory itself. Either all the sources are remote and the target is local, or the reverse; copies from one remote machine to another are refused.

If the target is a directory, the files go into it. With several sources the target must be a directory. A remote name may end in a pattern with `*` or `?`, which the server expands (quote it so that the local shell leaves it alone). A directory needs *-r*. The files are made with the mode they had (the local umask is not applied to the remote side).

On a terminal each file shows a progress line; *-q* hides it. The login works as in ssh(1), including the questions about unknown hosts and passwords; *-B* asks nothing.

# OPTIONS
@ *-r*
    Copy directories, with everything in them.
@ *-p*
    Keep the modification times and modes of the originals.
@ *-P* _PORT_
    The port (a capital P, as for sftp; ssh uses a small p).
@ *-i* _FILE_, *-o* _OPTION_, *-F* _FILE_
    As for ssh(1).
@ *-q*, *-B*, *-v*
    No progress meter; never ask (BatchMode); more detail.

# EXIT STATUS
0; 1 if something could not be copied (each failure is printed, the rest is still copied); 255 if the connection could not be made.

# EXAMPLES
    scp notes.txt marisa@gensokyo:
    scp -r photos marisa@gensokyo:/HumanVillage/marisa/
    scp 'marisa@gensokyo:/tmp/*.log' .

# SEE ALSO
sftp(1), ssh(1), sftp-server(8)
